  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Boletopsis grisea, commonly known as the grey falsebolete, is a wood-decaying fungus distinguished by its grey to brownish cap and pores rather than gills on its underside. Despite its bolete-like appearance, it belongs to the Boletopsidaceae family, making it a 'false' bolete structurally different from true boletes.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Distinctive grey to dark grey or brownish cap with a matte, often felty or slightly wrinkled surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "Angular, decurrent (running down the stem) pores that are grey to brown and often appear somewhat waxy or soft",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Thick, sturdy stem that is grey or brownish, typically lacking a ring or significant coloration changes",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Boletopsis grisea grows on decaying wood, logs, and woody debris in coniferous and mixed forests throughout New England, preferring moist conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite resembling true boletes with its pored underside, Boletopsis grisea is structurally distinct enough to warrant its own family, making it an example of convergent evolution in fungal morphology.",
